{
  "dom_id": "step-q7484",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219884/assets/d1fd1460fe24739d1c1bf1a7cad39da6775860b0764eaa4b41fffaae073e3a50.png)\n\nGiven the picture shown above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $E$, $G$ and $H$ are collinear\n\nII. $A$, $B$ and $G$ are **not** collinear\n\nIII. $B$, $F$ and $H$ are collinear",
  "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219884/assets/fb82255ac578efc90bb070dab669a19950387cab7d9011de4d5d0d94dd9de505.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219884/assets/2c20ebe076b3e655b7709ccb337c68d4aa8505bfabdb5a8755e5a1410642d612.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219884/assets/9859ea0144923d69e37d64130910608f6fc3e7afe09d74af19a5402030e0d3cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
